      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "From 1916 to 1922, the Uniontown Speedway board track, backed by coal barons and Carl Laemmle, drew racing crowds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniontown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "On April 9, 2024, the Abel Colley Tavern Museum opened, offering visitors a book, puppet, and Fayette County history experience.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://fayettehistoricalsociety.org/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Fort Necessity, just 10 miles southeast, was built by George Washington during the French and Indian War and was the site of the Battle of Jumonville Glen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniontown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The USS Uniontown, a Tacoma‑class frigate renamed from Chattanooga in 1944, was the only U.S. Navy ship named after the city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
